          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Nicole:</strong> Vulnerability, Threat, Risk, Attack are major terms that you will often hear used in cyber security.</p>
<p>Let’s have a look at these in more detail.</p>
<p>A risk is simply defined as a negative event. In terms of cybersecurity an example of a negative event is your computer being hacked. For there to be a risk, you need to have two things: a threat and a vulnerability.</p>
<p>If we have lots of threats such as lots of criminals that want to hack you, but no vulnerabilities such as weaknesses in your cybersecurity then there is no risk. Alternatively, if you have lots of vulnerabilities, but no threats, there is no risk! Well for now that is.</p>
<p>In cyber security because we often can’t control the threats from those who want to cause us harm, we must focus on reducing our vulnerabilities to as close to zero as possible.</p>
<p>A vulnerability is a weakness that can be exploited by cybercriminals to gain unauthorised access to a computer system. Systems that don't have adequate cybersecurity leave themselves open to multiple threats opportunities from cybercriminals. Having up-to-date Antimalware software can help to cut down the threat and reduce vulnerability, but also being aware of any threats, how they affect the user, can help inform them on how to be more secure in their cyber activity. Understanding the different types of threats and how to defend against them is imperative for cyber security in a business or organisation.</p>
<p>All businesses and organisations are at risk of cyberattack and should be fully aware of their exposure or resulting loss. It's not just the potential effects of data breach or loss it is the long-term harm to technical infrastructure and reputation of an organisation.</p>
<p>To work out any cybersecurity risk an organisation or company should identify and document any asset vulnerabilities, assess their system vulnerabilities, identify the impact to the business and prioritise risk responses.</p>
<p>They should also identify any potential risk of attack from insider or external threats and any global trends that signify that they may be at greater risk of attack.</p>
<p>By having a cyber security strategy in place an organisation can be prepared to act if an attack should happen but more importantly be ready and protected from that attack.</p>
<p>Having up to date cyber security is key to that strategy and having a dedicated team who are prepared for any threats with clear guidelines in place on how to handle that threat means that they should be able to put their strategies into play, should it occur. If another organisation has suffered a cyber attack then it is only a matter of time before the threat actors may try again. They may already have malware lying dormant within the network systems.</p>
<p>Having a disaster recovery plan [DRP] can help to redirect available resources into restoring data and information systems. DRPs can provide instructions to follow in the event of an attack which in turn may minimise the severity.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
